<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and y-Intercept</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00253</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.12.2: Writing an Equation Given the Slope and y-Intercept</md:title>
    <md:uuid>fdcab753-729d-44e7-a3c1-c4a328aa455a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>We can  determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \( y = mx + b \), where \(m\) is the slope and \( b \) is the \( y \)-intercept. Now we will do the reverse—we will start with the slope and \(y\)-intercept and use them to write the equation of the line.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Students found the slope of a line using the formula. Use a graph to demonstrate that the slope of the line is the ratio of rise over run, change in \( y \) over change in \( x \). Ask students: </para>
<list>
  <item>How can you identify the \( y \)-intercept from a graph? (It is the point where the line crosses the \( y \)-axis.) </item>
  <item>What is the value of \( x \) where the line crosses the \( y \)-axis? (0)  </item>
</list>

<title>Student Activity</title>

<para><link url="https://teacher.desmos.com/activitybuilder/custom/64a712cbf03563b68f6514e2 ">Desmos Activity: RAISE 1.12.2 Writing an Equation Given the Slope and \(y\)-Intercept</link></para>


<para>This activity uses multiple approaches to review the identification of the slope and \(y\)-intercept from an equation given in slope-intercept form.</para>
<list>
  <item>
    Use the Desmos Teacher Dashboard to monitor student responses for screens 1 - 3.
  </item>
  <item>
    Screen 4, or question 4, asks students to match the correct slope and \(y\)-intercept with an equation using a digital card sort.
  </item>
  <item>
    Screen 5, or question 5, asks students to write an equation given the slope and \(y\)-intercept. For this question, students are given the choice to share their response with the class. This allows students to compare their own response with the one submitted by others. Follow up with a whole-class discussion.
  </item>
  <item>
    Screen 6 repeats the process of asking students to write an equation given the slope and \(y\)-intercept - but it also asks students to explain their reasoning. Follow up with a whole-class discussion.
  </item>
</list>

<para>Use the whole-class discussions to help students develop a connection between the equation of a line and the features of its graph. If you would like, arrange the students into partners and allow them to work together on this activity.</para>

<para>Desmos activities are different from the graphing calculator application because they lead students through one or more interactive tasks. As students progress through the activity, teachers may use the Teacher Dashboard to monitor student responses and use them for whole group discussion. Activities may also be set to identify student responses that are correct or incorrect.</para>
<div class="os-raise-familysupport">
  <para>Access the <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e5b8beffe7473ad7fdafa2144d654c86bfe0e451">Desmos guide PDF</link> to learn more about accessing and customizing activities.</para>
</div>

<para><emphasis>Desmos Questions</emphasis></para>
<para>The slope-intercept form of the equation of a line is</para>
<para>\(y = mx + b\)</para>
<para>where m is the slope and b is the \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>&amp;vltri; The \(y\)-intercept is where the graph crosses the \(y\)-axis.</para>
<para>&amp;vltri; For \(y\)-intercept b, the coordinates of the point are \((0, b)\).</para>

<para>Screen 1 -</para>
<para>What is the slope, m, of the line represented by the equation, \(y = 2x + 5\)?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<para>What is the \(y\)-intercept, \(b\), of the line represented by the equation, \(y = 2x + 5\)?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5</para>

<para>Screen 2 -</para>
<para>\(y = -3x + 2\)</para>
<para>Sketch a<emphasis> blue circle </emphasis>around the slope, \(m\), of the given equation for a line.</para>
<para>Sketch a <emphasis>yellow circle </emphasis>around the \(y\)-intercept, \(b\), of the given equation for a line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -3x + 2\); -3 should be circled in blue; 2 should be circled in yellow.</para>

<para>Screen 3 -</para>
<para>What is the slope,\(m\), of the line represented by the equation, \(y = -\frac15x - 1\)?</para>
<list>
  <item>
    -1
  </item>
  <item>
    1
  </item>
  <item>
    \(\frac15\)
  </item>
  <item>
    \(-\frac15\)
  </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(-\frac15\)</para>
<para>What is the \(y\)-intercept, \(b\), of the line represented by the equation, \(y =-\frac15x - 1\)?</para>
<list>
  <item>
    -1
  </item>
  <item>
    1
  </item>
  <item>
  \(\frac15\) </item>
  <item>\(-\frac15\) </item>
</list>

<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -1</para>

<para>Screen 4 -</para>
<para>Digital card sort matching equations with the correct slope and \(y\)-intercept.</para>
<para>\(y = -2x + 3\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Slope \(= -2\); \(y\)-intercept \(= 3\)</para>

<para>\(y = 3x - 2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Slope \(= 3\); \(y\)-intercept \(= -2\)</para>

<para>\(y = 2x - 3\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Slope \(= 2\); \(y\)-intercept \(= -3\)</para>

<para>\(y = -\frac12x - 3\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Slope \(= -\frac12\); \(y\)-intercept \(= -3\)</para>

<para>Screen 5 -</para>
<para>Write the equation of a line that has a slope of -9 and a \(y\)-intercept at \((0, -4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = -9x - 4\)</para>

<para>Screen 6 -</para>
<para>Write the equation of a line that has a slope of 25 and a \(y\)-intercept at \((0, 4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y = \frac25x + 4\)</para>


    <title>Activity Synthesis</title>
    <para>The slope-intercept form of the equation of a line is the most commonly used form. You can identify the slope and the \( y \)-intercept, and it is the form used to graph the line using a graphing calculator. The equation \( y=mx+b \) may look to some students like there are 4 variables. Point out the \( m \) and \( b \) are numbers that you are given to substitute into the equation. The value of \( m \) is the coefficient of \( x \), and the value of \( b \) is the constant in the equation of the line.</para>
    <para>Students may be confused about the value of \( b \) as the \( y \)-intercept. Elicit that the \( y \)-intercept is the point where the graph crosses the \( y \)-axis. The \( x \)-value of the \( y \)-intercept is zero. In the slope-intercept form, the \( b \) represents the \(y\)-value of the \( y \)-intercept. Sometimes the \( y \)-intercept is identified as just the \( y \)-value. For example, the \( y \)-intercept is 2 really means the \( y \)-intercept is \( (0, 2) \). To write the equation of a line in slope-intercept form, substitute 2 for \( b \): \( y=mx+2 \). </para>
    <para>It may help students to practice writing equations with the same slope and different \(y\)-intercepts. For example, have students write the equation of a line with slope 2 and \( y \)-intercept \( (0, 4) \) \( (y=2x+4) \); slope 2 and \( y \)-intercept \( (0, -1) \) \( (y=2x-1) \); slope 2 and \( y \)-intercept \((0, 3)\) \( (y=2x+3) \). Then have students write the equation of a line with different slopes and the same \( y \)-intercept. For example, have students write the equation of a line with slope 4 and \( y \)-intercept 5; slope \( \frac{2}{3} \) and \( y \)-intercept 5; slope \( -\frac{1}{2} \) and \( y \)-intercept 5. </para>
    <title>1.12.2: Self Check </title>
  

<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>  
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para>Write the equation of a line in slope-intercept form with the slope of \( -\frac{3}{4} \) and a \( y \)-intercept of \( -2 \).</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
   <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>Answers</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Feedback</emphasis></entry></row>
 </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = - \frac{3}{4} x - 2 \)</entry>
      <entry>That’s correct! Check yourself: The slope-intercept form is \( y=mx+b \). The slope is \( m= -\frac{3}{4} \), and the \(y\)-intercept is \( b=-2 \), so this is the correct equation.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = -\frac{3}{4} x + 2 \)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope-intercept form of the equation of a line is \( y=mx+b \). Be careful with the signs during substitution. The answer is \( y = - \frac{3}{4} x - 2 \).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( 3x + 4y =-8 \)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: Make sure to use the correct form. This is standard form, not slope-intercept form. The answer is \( y = - \frac{3}{4} x - 2 \).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>\( y = \frac{3}{4} x -2 \)</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The slope-intercept form for the equation of a line is \( y=mx+b \). Be careful with the signs during substitution. The answer is \( y = - \frac{3}{4} x - 2 \).</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
 
<title>1.12.2: Additional Resources </title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it. </emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
<para>We can determine the slope and intercept of a line if the equation is written in slope-intercept form, \( y=mx+b \). Now we will do the reverse—we will start with the slope and \( y \)-intercept and use them to find the equation of the line.</para>
<para><emphasis>Example 1 </emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of a line with slope \( −1 \) and \( y \)-intercept \( (0,−3) \).</para>
<para><emphasis>Solution </emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis> - Name the slope. 
\( m = -1 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Name the \( y \)-intercept. 
\( b = -3 \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \).
\( y = -1x + -3 \) 
\( y = -x-3 \) </para>
<para>
The same information can be picked up from a graph of a line. </para>
<para><emphasis>Example 2 </emphasis></para>
<para>Find the equation of the line shown in the graph.</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2aa4ff4aed47d92a6011b39628987c2fa8ee501a"/></media></figure></para>
<para> <emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
\( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
From \( (0, -4) \) to \( (3,-2) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{2}{3} \). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
\( y \)-intercept \( (0, -4) \)
\( b = -4 \) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b \). 
\( y = mx +b \)
\( y = \frac{2}{3} x - 4 \)</para>

<title>Try It: Write Equations Given a Slope and \( y \)-Intercept</title>
<para>Write the equation of the line graphed below in slope-intercept form:</para>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/a2d136d8b31fa1842f0d6e000e386cbe46767603"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Write down your answer, and then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<h6>Answer</h6>
<para>Here is how to write the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Find the slope by counting the rise and run.
  \( m=\frac{rise}{run} \) 
From \( (0, 1) \) to \( (5, 4) \), the slope is \( m = \frac{3}{5} \). </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the \( y \)-intercept. 
\( y \)-intercept \( (0, 1) \)
\( b = 1 \) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Substitute the values into \( y = mx + b\).
\( y = mx +b \)
\( y = \frac{3}{5} x + 1 \)</para>
</section>
